# The ground of Python: the gifts of the language and its standard library that programs commonly lean on, one entry
# each (MacroCOSM OES 18.4.9, "The ground"; Thym 20.10, `ground repo with f`). Read by enough/languages.py; applied by
# the move `ground` (enough/thym/moves/transconceive.py), which copies an entry whole into a fabulac as a C:reliance
# (or a P:assumption) for every entry whose `shows-as` the program's syntax shows, with `entry` naming the entry.
#
# An entry: `id`; `kind` (reliance, the default, or assumption); `on` (language, runtime, platform, dependency); `of`
# (means: the language itself; runtime: its runtime and standard library; else a package's name); `name`; `gives`
# and `example`, in plain words that name neither the language nor a dependency; `names`, the patterns a fabulac's
# words would name this gift by (an item whose summary, means or gives matches is listed in the reliance's `used-by`);
# `shows-as`, alternatives of conditions on one call or one form of a file (any alternative holding raises the entry);
# `usual`, the ruling it usually takes; `stated`, where the gift is written, each `{in, version, at, digest}` (`digest`
# the first twelve hex digits of the sha256 of the cited page of the publisher's plain-text build) or `{nowhere}`;
# `from`, where the entry came from: the paper's fourteen (`paper`), the experiment's reliances (`V-R..`, `S-R..`),
# the 2026-11-05 trial's writers' notes (`TS 3.n`, `SW 3.n`: section 3, "Assumed knowledge", item n).
#
# Copyright © 2026 Graham Smith. CC BY 4.0, with a patent promise under OWFa 1.0. See defaults/cosm/NOTICE.md.
ground: ground/1
language: python
title: The ground of Python
version: '3.13'
about: >-
  What a program written in Python takes from its language, its runtime and its standard library without saying so.
  Each citation names the Python documentation of the 3.13 series as the language library keeps it (its plain-text
  build of 3.13.18), by page and the publisher's anchor.
texts:
  - shelf: python
    text: docs
    version: '3.13'
    name: The Python documentation
    publisher: Python Software Foundation
    what: text
    form: text
    lane: open
    aboard: true
    licence: {name: 'PSF License Version 2; examples, recipes and other code in the documentation are also under the
        Zero-Clause BSD license', url: 'https://docs.python.org/3.13/license.html'}
    from: https://docs.python.org/3.13/archives/python-3.13-docs-text.tar.bz2
    cited-as: python
    cited-version: 3.13.18
pins:
  - shelf: python
    text: cpython
    version: '3.13'
    name: CPython
    publisher: Python Software Foundation
    what: implementation
    lane: open
    aboard: false
    licence: {name: PSF License Version 2, url: 'https://docs.python.org/3.13/license.html'}
    from: https://github.com/python/cpython
    release: null
    swhid: null
    digest: null
entries:

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Numbers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- id: whole-numbers-unbounded
  on: language
  of: means
  name: whole numbers without limit
  gives: Whole numbers have no fixed width. Adding, subtracting and multiplying them never overflows and never loses a
    digit, however large they grow.
  example: 18446744073709551615 plus 1 is 18446744073709551616, exactly; a total past two to the sixty-third is kept.
  shows-as: [{call: int}, {annotation: int}, {method: add_argument, kw: {type: int}}]
  usual: kept
  from: [paper, S-R09, TS 1.11, SW 1.10, SW 1.34]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#numeric-types-int-float-complex, digest: 736b53f803b0}

- id: number-from-text
  on: language
  of: means
  name: a whole number read from text
  gives: A whole number is read from text that may have spaces, tabs or line ends around it, a plus or minus sign,
    single underscores between digits, leading zeros, and the decimal digits of any script, not only 0 to 9. Anything
    else is refused.
  example: "' 5_0', '+7', '007' and the Arabic-Indic digit seven are read as 50, 7, 7 and 7; '5__0' and '1.0' are refused."
  names: ['\bint\(\)']
  shows-as: [{call: int}, {method: add_argument, kw: {type: int}}]
  usual: kept
  from: [paper, V-R04, TS 3.14, SW 3.13, TS 1.10, SW 1.9]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#int, digest: 216dc071d8fc}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#numeric-types-int-float-complex, digest: 736b53f803b0}

- id: halves-round-to-even
  on: language
  of: means
  name: halves round to the even neighbour
  gives: Rounding a number to a whole number takes the nearer whole number, and a number exactly halfway goes to the
    even one. The result is a whole number.
  example: 2.5 rounds to 2, 3.5 to 4, and -0.5 to 0.
  shows-as: [{call: round}]
  usual: kept
  from: [paper, TS 3.17, SW 3.19, TS 1.38, SW 1.39]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#round, digest: 216dc071d8fc}

- id: division-gives-fraction
  on: language
  of: means
  name: dividing gives a fraction
  gives: Dividing one whole number by another with the plain division sign gives a fractional number, correctly
    rounded, even when the division is exact.
  example: 6 divided by 3 is 2.0, not 2; 1 divided by 3 is 0.3333333333333333.
  shows-as: [{form: true-division}]
  usual: kept
  from: [SW 3.19]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: reference/expressions.html#binary-arithmetic-operations, digest: 9188471e6a0d}

- id: division-floors
  on: language
  of: means
  name: whole-number division rounds down
  gives: Whole-number division rounds toward minus infinity, not toward zero, and the remainder takes the sign of the
    divisor.
  example: -7 divided by 2 in whole numbers is -4, remainder 1.
  shows-as: [{form: floor-division}]
  usual: kept
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: reference/expressions.html#binary-arithmetic-operations, digest: 9188471e6a0d}

- id: fractions-are-doubles
  on: language
  of: means
  name: fractional numbers are binary doubles
  gives: A fractional number is a 64-bit binary floating-point number. Not-a-number is unequal to everything, itself
    included; there is a positive and a negative zero; a literal too large to hold reads as infinity.
  example: 0.1 plus 0.2 is 0.30000000000000004; 1e400 is infinity.
  shows-as: [{call: float}, {annotation: float}, {call: time.time}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R10, SW 1.29]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#numeric-types-int-float-complex, digest: 736b53f803b0}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: tutorial/floatingpoint.html#floating-point-arithmetic-issues-and-limitations,
       digest: 783ed86151eb}

- id: fraction-from-text
  on: language
  of: means
  name: a fractional number read from text
  gives: A fractional number is read from text with spaces around it, a sign, underscores between digits, an exponent,
    and the words nan, inf and infinity in any case.
  example: "' -1_0.5e1 ' reads as -105.0; 'Infinity' reads as infinity."
  shows-as: [{call: float}]
  usual: kept
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#float, digest: 216dc071d8fc}

- id: number-as-text
  on: language
  of: means
  name: numbers written as text
  gives: A whole number is written in decimal digits with a minus sign when negative. A fractional number is written
    with the fewest digits that read back as the same number, always with a point or an exponent; from 1e16 up and
    below 1e-4 it takes the exponent form, with a sign and at least two digits.
  example: 1.0 is written 1.0, not 1; 1e16 is written 1e+16; 0.0001 is written 0.0001 and 0.00001 is written 1e-05.
  shows-as: [{call: float}, {annotation: float}, {call: time.time}, {call: str}, {form: f-string}]
  usual: kept
  from: [paper, TS 3.16, SW 3.17, TS 1.20, TS 1.21, SW 1.29]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#repr, digest: 216dc071d8fc}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: tutorial/floatingpoint.html#floating-point-arithmetic-issues-and-limitations,
       digest: 783ed86151eb}
    - nowhere: the exact switch to the exponent form (from 1e16 up, below 1e-4) and the two-digit exponent.

- id: conversions
  on: language
  of: means
  name: values converted to a kind
  gives: Converting a value to a whole number drops a fraction toward zero and takes yes as 1 and no as 0; converting
    text reads it as a number (see the entries on numbers read from text); converting anything to text gives its
    printed form. A value that cannot be converted is refused with a value error or a type error.
  example: a fraction 2.9 converted to a whole number is 2; yes is 1; the text '3' converted is 3; a list is refused.
  shows-as: [{call: int}, {call: float}, {call: str}, {call: bool}]
  usual: kept
  from: [TS 1.22, SW 1.17]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#int, digest: 216dc071d8fc}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#float, digest: 216dc071d8fc}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#bool, digest: 216dc071d8fc}

- id: value-kinds
  on: language
  of: means
  name: a value's kind and its name
  gives: A value's kind can be asked for and compared exactly. Yes and no are a kind of whole number when asked loosely,
    but not when the kind is compared exactly. A kind's name is its short word (int, str, list, bool, float).
  example: yes is a whole number when asked loosely; the kind of yes is not the kind of 1; the name of the kind of 1 is int.
  shows-as: [{call: type}, {call: isinstance}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R09]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#type, digest: 216dc071d8fc}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#isinstance, digest: 216dc071d8fc}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#bool, digest: 216dc071d8fc}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#definition.__name__, digest: 736b53f803b0}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Text
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- id: text-length-in-code-points
  on: language
  of: means
  name: text measured in code points
  gives: The length of a text, and every padding to a width, counts Unicode code points, not bytes, not UTF-16 units and
    not the columns a terminal draws. A wide character or a combining mark counts as one.
  example: "'café' is 4 long; padded to 6 it gains two spaces; a name of three CJK ideographs counts 3."
  shows-as: [{method: ljust}, {method: rjust}, {method: center}, {form: format-width}, {call: len}]
  usual: kept
  from: [paper, S-R04, TS 3.19, SW 3.22, TS 1.36, SW 1.37]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#text-sequence-type-str, digest: 736b53f803b0}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#str.ljust, digest: 736b53f803b0}

- id: text-indexed-by-code-point
  on: language
  of: means
  name: positions in text count code points
  gives: A position in a text, a slice of it and a match's start and end count Unicode code points from 0. A slice past
    either end is cut short, never refused; a negative position counts from the end.
  example: in 'é𝒳x' the x is at position 2; the slice from 1 to 99 of 'abc' is 'bc'.
  shows-as: [{form: slice}, {method: find}, {method: rfind}, {method: start}, {method: end}, {method: span}]
  usual: kept
  from: [S-R04]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#text-sequence-type-str, digest: 736b53f803b0}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#common-sequence-operations, digest: 736b53f803b0}

- id: text-order-by-code-point
  on: language
  of: means
  name: text ordered by code point
  gives: Texts are ordered by comparing code point by code point, not by any language's alphabet and not by locale, so
    every capital letter of A to Z comes before every small letter, and a shorter text before a longer one it begins.
  example: sorted, 'B', 'a', 'é' and 'b' come out as 'B', 'a', 'b', 'é'.
  names: ['\bsorted\(\)']
  shows-as: [{call: sorted}, {method: sort}, {call: max}, {call: min}]
  usual: kept
  from: [paper, V-R14, TS 3.20, SW 3.22, TS 1.41, SW 1.35]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: reference/expressions.html#value-comparisons, digest: 9188471e6a0d}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#sorted, digest: 216dc071d8fc}

- id: sort-is-stable
  on: language
  of: means
  name: sorting keeps ties in order
  gives: Sorting is stable. Things that compare equal under the sort's key keep the order they had before.
  example: sorted by length, 'bb', 'a', 'c' give 'a', 'c', 'bb'.
  shows-as: [{call: sorted}, {method: sort}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R14]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#sorted, digest: 216dc071d8fc}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#list.sort, digest: 736b53f803b0}

- id: what-is-a-space
  on: language
  of: means
  name: what counts as white space
  gives: White space is every character Unicode's database classes as a space separator or as a bidirectional space,
    segment or paragraph separator. That is the ASCII space, tab, line feed, vertical tab, form feed and carriage
    return, the four information separators U+001C to U+001F, U+0085, U+00A0, U+1680, U+2000 to U+200A, U+2028,
    U+2029, U+202F, U+205F and U+3000. Trimming, splitting on white space and asking whether a text is white space all
    use this one set.
  example: a name of 'tea' followed by U+3000 is trimmed to 'tea'; 'a', U+001F, 'b' holds white space.
  names: ['\bisspace\b', '\bstrip\(\)']
  shows-as: [{method: strip}, {method: lstrip}, {method: rstrip}, {method: isspace}, {method: split, without: [sep]}]
  usual: kept
  from: [paper, V-R17, S-R03, TS 3.18, SW 3.20, TS 1.15, TS 1.25, SW 1.14, SW 1.16]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#str.isspace, digest: 736b53f803b0}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#str.strip, digest: 736b53f803b0}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#str.split, digest: 736b53f803b0}

- id: text-split-on-space
  on: language
  of: means
  name: text split into words
  gives: Splitting a text with no separator given splits on runs of white space and drops empty pieces, so a text of
    only white space gives no words; splitting with a separator keeps every empty piece.
  example: "'  a  b ' split gives 'a' and 'b'; 'a,,b' split at commas gives 'a', '' and 'b'."
  shows-as: [{method: split}, {method: rsplit}]
  usual: kept
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#str.split, digest: 736b53f803b0}

- id: lines-end-at
  on: language
  of: means
  name: where a line ends
  gives: A text split into lines breaks at line feed, carriage return, carriage return then line feed (one break), line
    tabulation, form feed, the file, group and record separators U+001C to U+001E, U+0085, U+2028 and U+2029. The
    breaks are not kept, and a final break does not make an empty last line.
  example: "'a', U+2028, 'b' is two lines; 'a\\r\\nb\\n' is two lines, 'a' and 'b'."
  names: ['\bsplitlines\b']
  shows-as: [{method: splitlines}]
  usual: kept
  from: [paper, V-R18, TS 1.24, SW 3.21, SW 1.15]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#str.splitlines, digest: 736b53f803b0}

- id: text-case-mapping
  on: language
  of: means
  name: changing the case of text
  gives: Lower-casing, upper-casing and case folding use Unicode's full mappings, so a character may become several and
    the length may change; they ignore the locale.
  example: upper-cased, 'ß' becomes 'SS'; folded, 'ẞ' and 'ss' are equal; 'İ' lower-cased is 'i' and a combining dot.
  shows-as: [{method: lower}, {method: upper}, {method: casefold}, {method: title}, {method: capitalize}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R14]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#str.lower, digest: 736b53f803b0}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#str.casefold, digest: 736b53f803b0}

- id: text-replace
  on: language
  of: means
  name: replacing in text
  gives: Replacing a piece of text replaces every occurrence, scanning left to right, without overlap, unless a count is
    given; nothing found leaves the text as it was.
  example: replacing 'aa' by 'b' in 'aaa' gives 'ba'.
  shows-as: [{method: replace}]
  usual: kept
  from: [S-R11, S-R13]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#str.replace, digest: 736b53f803b0}

- id: text-repeated
  on: language
  of: means
  name: text repeated
  gives: A text repeated a whole number of times is that many copies joined; zero or a negative number of times gives
    the empty text, never an error.
  example: "'#' three times is '###'; '#' minus two times is ''."
  shows-as: [{form: text-repeat}]
  usual: kept
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#common-sequence-operations, digest: 736b53f803b0}

- id: code-points-by-number
  on: language
  of: means
  name: characters by number
  gives: A character is made from any number from 0 to 1114111 (U+10FFFF); a larger or negative number is refused with
    a value error. Text may hold any of them, the null character and lone surrogates (U+D800 to U+DFFF) included.
  example: number 55296 gives the lone surrogate U+D800, which the text keeps; 1114112 is refused.
  names: ['\bchr\(\)']
  shows-as: [{call: chr}, {call: ord}]
  usual: kept
  from: [S-R09, S-R10]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#chr, digest: 216dc071d8fc}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#ord, digest: 216dc071d8fc}

- id: printed-form
  on: language
  of: means
  name: a value's printed form
  gives: Any value has a printed form. Text prints as itself; bytes print as b followed by the quoted bytes; a quoted
    form of text (used in many messages) puts it in single quotes, or double quotes when it holds a single quote and no
    double one, with backslash escapes for what cannot be shown.
  example: the bytes of '<r>' print as b'<r>'; the quoted form of x is 'x', of it's is "it's".
  shows-as: [{call: str}, {call: repr}, {form: conversion-r}, {method: add_argument, kw: [type]}]
  usual: kept
  from: [S-R18, TS 3.5, SW 3.14]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#str, digest: 736b53f803b0}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#repr, digest: 216dc071d8fc}
    - nowhere: the rule for choosing single or double quotes in the quoted form of text, and which characters it escapes.

- id: format-spec
  on: language
  of: means
  name: the format of a value written into text
  gives: A value written into text may carry a format of fill, alignment, sign, width, grouping, precision and type.
    A plus sign shows the sign of positive numbers too; a width pads to that many code points; a fraction's precision
    rounds half to even.
  example: 1 formatted with a sign is '+1' and -2 is '-2'; 3 right-aligned in width 4 is '   3'.
  shows-as: [{form: format-spec}, {call: format}]
  usual: kept
  from: [TS 3.28, SW 2.25]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/string.html#format-specification-mini-language, digest: b1490c87ba39}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: reference/lexical_analysis.html#2-4-3-f-strings,
       digest: 26b7f8ab2fcf}

- id: percent-format
  on: language
  of: means
  name: text formatted with percent fields
  gives: Text with percent fields (a percent sign, an optional name in brackets, flags, width, precision and a letter)
    is filled from a value or a map of names; two percent signs give one.
  example: "'%(n)s items' filled with n = 3 gives '3 items'."
  shows-as: [{form: percent-format}]
  usual: kept
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#printf-style-string-formatting, digest: 736b53f803b0}

- id: format-string-fields
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: the fields of a format string
  gives: Brace fields in a format string are found by its grammar, a name followed by attributes and keys, a conversion
    after an exclamation mark and a format after a colon; two braces are an escaped brace; a malformed string gives no
    fields and an error.
  example: "'{a.b[0]!r:>4}' names the field a; '{{a}}' names none."
  shows-as: [{call: string.Formatter}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R21]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/string.html#format-string-syntax, digest: b1490c87ba39}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/string.html#string.Formatter.parse, digest: b1490c87ba39}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Truth, collections, records
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- id: truth-of-values
  on: language
  of: means
  name: which values count as false
  gives: Where a value is tested as a condition, zero of any kind, empty text, an empty list, map or set, and nothing
    count as false; everything else counts as true unless it says otherwise.
  example: a line holding only spaces, trimmed, is empty and so false; 0.0 is false; a list holding one empty text is true.
  shows-as: [{form: truth-test}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R10]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#truth-value-testing, digest: 736b53f803b0}

- id: boolean-operators-give-operands
  on: language
  of: means
  name: and, or give one of their operands
  gives: '"and" and "or" stop as soon as the answer is known and give the operand that decided it, not a plain yes or
    no: "a or b" is a when a counts as true, else b.'
  example: an empty name or 'anonymous' gives 'anonymous'.
  shows-as: [{form: or-default}]
  usual: kept
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: reference/expressions.html#boolean-operations, digest: 9188471e6a0d}

- id: any-all-stop-early
  on: language
  of: means
  name: any and all stop at the first answer
  gives: Asking whether any of a series holds stops at the first that does; asking whether all hold stops at the first
    that does not; the rest are never looked at. Any of nothing is false and all of nothing is true.
  example: of handlers checked in order, the second is never examined when the first already answers.
  names: ['\bany\(\)']
  shows-as: [{call: any}, {call: all}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R11]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#any, digest: 216dc071d8fc}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#all, digest: 216dc071d8fc}

- id: map-keeps-order
  on: language
  of: means
  name: a map of keys to values
  gives: A map from keys to values holds each key once and keeps the order in which keys were first put in; putting a
    key in again changes its value and keeps its place; a map built from sorted pairs stays sorted. Looking up a key it
    does not hold is refused with a key error unless a default is given.
  example: a="1" b="2" a="3" gives a=3 then b=2; the totals of b, a and c, put in sorted, come back a, b, c.
  names: ['\bdict(?:ionary)?\b']
  shows-as: [{form: map}, {call: dict}, {annotation: dict}]
  usual: kept
  from: [S-R11, S-R12, SW 1.19, TS 2.10, SW 2.14]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#mapping-types-dict, digest: 736b53f803b0}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#dict.get, digest: 736b53f803b0}

- id: set-members
  on: language
  of: means
  name: a set holds each value once
  gives: A set holds each value once, two values that compare equal (and hash alike) being one member; membership is
    asked in about constant time.
  example: two findings with the same file, line and name are one finding.
  shows-as: [{form: set}, {call: set}, {call: frozenset}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R13]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#set-types-set-frozenset, digest: 736b53f803b0}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: reference/datamodel.html#object.__hash__, digest: 95b8a5bdc225}

- id: set-order-varies
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: the order of a set of texts varies from run to run
  gives: Going through a set of texts visits them in an order that changes from one run of the program to the next,
    because the hash of a text is salted afresh at each start; only a sort makes it repeatable.
  example: the same three names, put in a set and printed, come out in a different order on another run.
  shows-as: [{form: set}, {call: set}, {call: frozenset}]
  usual: free
  from: [V-R14]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: reference/datamodel.html#object.__hash__, digest: 95b8a5bdc225}

- id: membership-identity-then-equality
  on: language
  of: means
  name: membership is identity first, then equality
  gives: Asking whether a value is among others asks, for each, whether it is that very object, then whether it is
    equal; the first yes answers.
  example: a constant compared against a list of constants by membership finds itself even if equality was redefined.
  shows-as: [{form: compare-in}]
  usual: kept
  from: [S-R19]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: reference/expressions.html#membership-test-operations, digest: 9188471e6a0d}

- id: records-compare-by-fields
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: records equal by their fields
  gives: A record type declared by its fields is equal to another of the same type when every field is equal; it can be
    unpacked in field order (when declared as a tuple), may be frozen against change, and fields may have defaults.
  example: two entries with the same time, name and amount are equal; a frozen entry cannot be changed.
  names: ['\bNamedTuple\b', '\bnamedtuple\b', '\bdataclass\w*']
  shows-as: [{decorator: dataclass}, {decorator: dataclasses.dataclass}, {base: NamedTuple},
             {base: typing.NamedTuple}, {call: collections.namedtuple}]
  usual: kept
  from: [S-R22, TS 2.5, SW 2.8]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/dataclasses.html#dataclasses.dataclass, digest: e1a82f4a2d34}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/typing.html#typing.NamedTuple, digest: fdda791ff698}

- id: lists-and-zip
  on: language
  of: means
  name: pairing lists stops at the shortest
  gives: Walking two series in step stops at the end of the shorter one, silently; the largest or smallest of nothing
    is refused unless a default is given.
  example: pairing 'a', 'b', 'c' with 1, 2 gives two pairs; the largest of no totals with a default of 0 is 0.
  shows-as: [{call: zip}, {call: max, kw: [default]}, {call: min, kw: [default]}]
  usual: kept
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#zip, digest: 216dc071d8fc}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#max, digest: 216dc071d8fc}

- id: generators-lazy
  on: language
  of: means
  name: a stream does nothing until asked
  gives: A function that yields its results one at a time checks its arguments when it is called, but runs none of its
    body until the first result is asked for, and then only as far as the next result.
  example: an unknown option is refused at the call; a bad input is found only when the first event is asked for.
  names: ['\bgenerator\b', '\byield\b']
  shows-as: [{form: yield}]
  usual: kept
  from: [S-R21]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: reference/expressions.html#yield-expressions, digest: 9188471e6a0d}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: reference/expressions.html#calls, digest: 9188471e6a0d}
    - nowhere: the wording of the message that refuses an unknown keyword argument.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Errors and exits
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- id: uncaught-error
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: an error nobody catches
  gives: An error the program does not catch ends it with exit status 1, after the runtime prints its report of the
    error (a traceback, the error's kind and message) on standard error.
  example: a ledger path that is a folder makes the program print a traceback and exit 1.
  names: ['\btraceback\b']
  shows-as: [{entry-point: true}]
  usual: alike
  from: [paper, V-R22, TS 3.26, SW 3.25, TS 1.27, SW 1.24]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/sys.html#sys.excepthook, digest: 1fc32ff781c0}
    - nowhere: that an uncaught error ends the process with exit status 1, and the traceback's exact form.

- id: error-text
  on: language
  of: means
  name: an error printed is its message
  gives: An error turned into text gives its message as it was raised, and the empty text when it was raised with none.
  example: an error raised with 'the ledger is broken' prints as that; one raised with no message prints as nothing.
  shows-as: [{form: handler-text}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R25, TS 2.4, SW 2.6]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/exceptions.html#BaseException, digest: fd1dcc489d5c}

- id: error-kinds
  on: language
  of: means
  name: errors caught by kind
  gives: A handler catches the kinds of error it names and every kind made from them. A text that is not valid JSON is
    a kind of value error; a missing key is a key error; a wrong kind of value is usually a type error; a missing file,
    a folder where a file was wanted and a refused permission are kinds of operating-system error.
  example: a line that is not JSON and a line missing a field are both caught by one handler naming value and key errors.
  shows-as: [{form: handler}]
  usual: kept
  from: [TS 1.22, SW 1.17]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/exceptions.html#exception-hierarchy, digest: fd1dcc489d5c}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/json.html#json.JSONDecodeError, digest: 12e3840498af}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: reference/compound_stmts.html#the-try-statement, digest: 4978b6e97f3d}

- id: exit-status-from-return
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: the exit status from a whole number
  gives: Ending the program with a whole number makes that number its exit status; ending it with nothing is status 0.
  example: a main step that returns 1 ends the program with status 1.
  shows-as: [{call: sys.exit}, {call: exit}, {call: SystemExit}]
  usual: kept
  from: [SW 3.26, TS 2.2, SW 2.2]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/sys.html#sys.exit, digest: 1fc32ff781c0}

- id: exit-with-message
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: exiting with a message
  gives: Ending the program with a message prints the message on standard error, followed by a line end, and exits
    with status 1.
  example: >-
    a missing path prints 'Error: m.py could not be found.' and exits 1.
  shows-as: [{call: sys.exit, arg0: str}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R23]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/sys.html#sys.exit, digest: 1fc32ff781c0}

- id: exit-status-whole-number
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: named statuses are whole numbers
  gives: A status named in an enumeration of whole numbers is that whole number wherever a number is wanted, the exit
    status included.
  example: a status named dead code found, valued 3, exits 3.
  names: ['\bIntEnum\b']
  shows-as: [{base: IntEnum}, {base: enum.IntEnum}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R33]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/enum.html#enum.IntEnum, digest: c29e837b8dbe}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/sys.html#sys.exit, digest: 1fc32ff781c0}

- id: chained-errors
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: an error raised from another
  gives: An error raised while another is handled, or raised from it, keeps the first as its cause; an uncaught one
    reports both, the cause first.
  example: a decode error turned into a ledger error prints both, when nobody catches the second.
  shows-as: [{form: raise-from}]
  usual: free
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: reference/simple_stmts.html#the-raise-statement, digest: cf5e83ddb366}

- id: optional-library
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: a library that may be absent
  gives: Loading a library that is not installed fails with an import error that the program can catch, so a part
    that needs it can be offered only where it is installed.
  example: the second tree builder is offered only where its library loads.
  shows-as: [{handler: ImportError}, {handler: ModuleNotFoundError}]
  usual: kept
  from: [S-R20]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/exceptions.html#ImportError, digest: fd1dcc489d5c}

- id: missing-key-error
  on: language
  of: means
  name: a missing key's error names the key
  gives: Looking up a key a map does not hold raises a key error whose text is the key's quoted form.
  example: looking up 'other' among the tree builders fails with the text 'other' in single quotes.
  shows-as: [{handler: KeyError}, {form: map-lookup}]
  usual: kept
  from: [S-R20]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/exceptions.html#KeyError, digest: fd1dcc489d5c}
    - nowhere: that the key error's message is the key's quoted form.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The command line, the environment, the clock
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- id: program-arguments
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: the program's own arguments
  gives: The program's own arguments are the words it was started with, after its name, as text; bytes that are not
    valid in the locale's encoding are kept as lone surrogates so that they round-trip.
  example: started as 'tool add tea 3', the arguments are 'add', 'tea' and '3'.
  names: ['\bsys\.argv\b']
  shows-as: [{attr: sys.argv}, {method: parse_args}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R03, SW 3.28, SW 1.50]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/sys.html#sys.argv, digest: 1fc32ff781c0}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/os.html#file-names-command-line-arguments-and-environment-variables,
       digest: 7df584c5ef74}

- id: argparse-surface
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: the option reader's errors and messages
  gives: 'A refused command line prints the usage line, then "PROG: error: MESSAGE", on standard error and exits with
    status 2. The messages are fixed phrases: "the following arguments are required: X", "unrecognized arguments: A B",
    "argument X: invalid choice: ''V'' (choose from a, b)" (the choices unquoted, as 3.13.11 was recorded;
    earlier releases quoted them),
    "argument X: invalid int value: ''V''", "argument
    X: ignored explicit argument ''V''", "ambiguous option: --x could match --xa, --xb". Values are shown in their
    quoted form. A sub-command''s errors name it ("tool add") and print its own usage line; words a sub-command does
    not take are reported by the top level.'
  example: 'started with no command, it prints "usage: tally [-h] [--version] {add,show,undo} ..." and "tally: error:
    the following arguments are required: command" and exits 2.'
  names: ['\bargparse\b']
  shows-as: [{call: argparse.ArgumentParser}]
  usual: kept
  from: [paper, V-R03, TS 3.1, TS 3.2, TS 3.3, TS 3.4, TS 3.5, TS 3.6, TS 3.9, SW 3.1, SW 3.2, SW 3.7, SW 3.8,
         SW 3.11, TS 1.3, TS 1.5, TS 1.8, TS 1.12, SW 1.7, SW 1.44, SW 1.47, SW 1.53]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/argparse.html#argparse.ArgumentParser.error, digest: bd7ccccdf95d}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/argparse.html#argparse.ArgumentParser.exit, digest: bd7ccccdf95d}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/argparse.html#prog, digest: bd7ccccdf95d}
    - nowhere: the exact wording of the option reader's messages and its usage line.

- id: argparse-help
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: the option reader's help screen
  gives: Asking for help prints, on standard output, the usage line, a blank line, the description, then "positional
    arguments:" and "options:" with each argument's help, indented two spaces, the help column at the longest
    invocation plus two (capped at 24), wrapped to the terminal's width; "-h, --help" says "show this help message and
    exit"; then it exits 0.
  example: '"tally -h" ends with "--version  show program''s version number and exit", and exits 0.'
  shows-as: [{call: argparse.ArgumentParser}]
  usual: alike
  from: [TS 3.11, TS 3.13, SW 3.3, SW 3.4, TS 1.1, SW 1.1, SW 1.2, SW 1.3, SW 1.52]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/argparse.html#add-help, digest: bd7ccccdf95d}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/argparse.html#formatter-class, digest: bd7ccccdf95d}
    - nowhere: the help screen's layout, its column widths, and that it wraps to the terminal's width.

- id: argparse-version
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: the version option
  gives: The version option prints the version text and a line end on standard output and exits 0, as soon as it is
    met, before any later word is checked.
  example: '"tally --version add" prints "tally 0.2.0" and exits 0; "tally add tea --version" is refused.'
  shows-as: [{method: add_argument, kw: {action: version}}]
  usual: kept
  from: [TS 3.12, SW 3.4, SW 3.7, TS 1.4]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/argparse.html#action, digest: bd7ccccdf95d}

- id: argparse-abbreviation
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: long options may be shortened
  gives: A long option may be given by any prefix that names only one option; a prefix naming several is refused as
    ambiguous.
  example: '"--vers" is read as "--version"; "--a" as "--all" when no other option begins so.'
  shows-as: [{call: argparse.ArgumentParser, without: [allow_abbrev]}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R03, TS 3.8, SW 3.10, TS 1.7, SW 1.43]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/argparse.html#allow-abbrev, digest: bd7ccccdf95d}

- id: argparse-negative-numbers
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: negative numbers are values
  gives: A word that looks like a negative number (minus then digits, with a point allowed) is taken as a value, not
    an option, while no option looks like one; a word that holds a space is always a value.
  example: '"tally add tea -2" adds -2; "tally add tea -1.5" is refused as an invalid whole number.'
  shows-as: [{method: add_argument, kw: {type: int}}, {method: add_argument, kw: {type: float}}]
  usual: kept
  from: [TS 3.7, SW 3.6, SW 1.48, SW 1.49]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/argparse.html#arguments-containing, digest: bd7ccccdf95d}

- id: argparse-optional-positional
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: an optional value is matched once
  gives: Values are matched to the arguments a run of plain words at a time; an optional value (zero or one) is matched
    in the first run even to nothing, so a plain word after an option in between is an unrecognised argument.
  example: '"tally add tea --x 3" reports "unrecognized arguments: --x 3".'
  shows-as: [{method: add_argument, kw: {nargs: '?'}}]
  usual: alike
  from: [TS 3.10, SW 3.9, TS 1.9, SW 1.45]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/argparse.html#nargs, digest: bd7ccccdf95d}
    - nowhere: that an optional value is matched in the first run of plain words, even to nothing.

- id: argparse-subcommands
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: sub-commands
  gives: A command made of sub-commands takes every word after the sub-command's name for that sub-command; a missing
    sub-command, when required, is reported as a required argument under its destination's name; an unknown one is an
    invalid choice; a first "--" ends the options.
  example: '"tally frob" reports "argument command: invalid choice: ''frob'' (choose from add, show, undo)", the choices unquoted.'
  shows-as: [{method: add_subparsers}]
  usual: kept
  from: [TS 3.3, SW 3.5, SW 3.7, SW 3.12, TS 1.6, SW 1.46]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/argparse.html#argparse.ArgumentParser.add_subparsers,
       digest: bd7ccccdf95d}
    - nowhere: how a "--" before the sub-command is read, which changed between releases.

- id: environment-read
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: the environment read as text
  gives: The environment is a map of names to text, read once when the program starts; a name that is not set is
    absent, which is told apart from one set to the empty text.
  example: with TALLY_FILE set to the empty text, the variable is present and empty.
  shows-as: [{attr: os.environ}, {call: os.getenv}, {call: os.environ.get}]
  usual: kept
  from: [TS 2.6, SW 2.11]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/os.html#os.environ, digest: 7df584c5ef74}

- id: clock-seconds
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: the time in seconds
  gives: The time now is a fractional number of seconds since 1970-01-01 at midnight UTC, with as much precision as
    the system gives (usually below a microsecond).
  example: a moment in November 2026 reads as about 1794000000.123456.
  shows-as: [{call: time.time}]
  usual: alike
  from: [TS 3.23, SW 3.18, SW 2.13]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/time.html#time.time, digest: 1c5e89faf7be}

- id: local-time
  on: platform
  of: runtime
  name: dates in the local time zone
  gives: A date or time made without a zone is in the machine's local time zone, and written by a format whose names of
    days and months follow the locale.
  example: the same moment is written as different hours on two machines in different zones.
  shows-as: [{call: datetime.datetime.now}, {call: time.localtime}, {call: time.strftime}, {method: strftime}]
  usual: alike
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/datetime.html#datetime.datetime.now, digest: 688281afa6bc}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/time.html#time.strftime, digest: 1c5e89faf7be}

- id: terminal-width
  on: platform
  of: runtime
  name: the terminal's width
  gives: The width of the terminal is read from the COLUMNS variable, else from the terminal, else taken as 80.
  example: with COLUMNS set to 40, help is wrapped to 40 columns.
  shows-as: [{call: shutil.get_terminal_size}, {call: argparse.ArgumentParser}]
  usual: free
  from: [V-R03]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/shutil.html#shutil.get_terminal_size, digest: 94ae9e1cf501}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- id: print-line-end
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: printing a line
  gives: Printing writes its values separated by one space and ends with one line feed, on standard output unless
    another stream is named; the stream is written in order and flushed when the program ends.
  example: >-
    printing 'tea: 3' writes 'tea: 3' and a line feed; an error printed to the error stream goes there.
  shows-as: [{call: print}]
  usual: kept
  from: [TS 3.29, SW 3.27, TS 1.43, SW 1.41]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#print, digest: 216dc071d8fc}

- id: output-encoding
  on: platform
  of: runtime
  name: the encoding of printed text
  gives: Printed text is encoded as UTF-8 on macOS and wherever the locale asks for UTF-8 or is the plain C locale;
    elsewhere in the locale's encoding, where a character that cannot be encoded stops the program with an error.
  example: a name with an ñ prints as UTF-8 bytes even with LANG=C.
  shows-as: [{call: print}, {attr: sys.stdout}, {attr: sys.stderr}]
  usual: alike
  from: [V-R32, TS 3.29, SW 3.27]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/os.html#python-utf-8-mode, digest: 7df584c5ef74}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/sys.html#sys.stdout, digest: 1fc32ff781c0}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# JSON
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- id: json-written
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: JSON as written
  gives: 'JSON is written on one line with ", " between items and ": " after keys; every character outside ASCII is
    written as a backslash, u and four lowercase hex digits (a pair of them for a character beyond U+FFFF), and
    control characters likewise, except the short escapes for quote, backslash, line feed, carriage return, tab,
    backspace and form feed; numbers are written as in the entry on numbers written as text; not-a-number and the
    infinities are written NaN, Infinity and -Infinity.'
  example: 'a name "café" with 3 is written {"café" written as "café": 3}; a time 1.5 is written 1.5.'
  shows-as: [{call: json.dumps}, {call: json.dump}]
  usual: kept
  from: [paper, TS 3.15, SW 3.15, TS 1.18, TS 1.19, SW 1.30, SW 1.31]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/json.html#json.dumps, digest: 12e3840498af}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/json.html#json.dump, digest: 12e3840498af}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/json.html#infinite-and-nan-number-values, digest: 12e3840498af}

- id: json-keys-sorted
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: JSON keys sorted
  gives: Asked to sort keys, JSON is written with each object's keys in code-point order.
  example: '{"name": "tea", "at": 1.0, "delta": 3} is written {"at": 1.0, "delta": 3, "name": "tea"}.'
  shows-as: [{call: json.dumps, kw: [sort_keys]}, {call: json.dump, kw: [sort_keys]}]
  usual: kept
  from: [TS 3.15, SW 3.15, SW 2.17]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/json.html#json.dumps, digest: 12e3840498af}

- id: json-read
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: JSON as read
  gives: JSON is read with white space (space, tab, line feed, carriage return) allowed around values; a number without
    a fraction or an exponent becomes a whole number of any size, exactly, any other a fractional number; NaN, Infinity
    and -Infinity are accepted; a raw control character inside a string is refused; a key given twice keeps its last
    value; anything else is refused with a decoding error, a kind of value error.
  example: '{"delta": 99999999999999999999} reads the delta exactly; {"a": 1, "a": 2} reads a = 2; {"at": NaN} is read.'
  shows-as: [{call: json.loads}, {call: json.load}]
  usual: kept
  from: [paper, TS 3.25, SW 3.16, TS 1.23, SW 1.19, SW 1.20]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/json.html#json.loads, digest: 12e3840498af}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/json.html#infinite-and-nan-number-values, digest: 12e3840498af}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/json.html#repeated-names-within-an-object, digest: 12e3840498af}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/json.html#json.JSONDecodeError, digest: 12e3840498af}

- id: csv-rows
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: comma-separated rows
  gives: Rows are written with fields quoted only when they must be, quotes doubled inside, and each row ended by a
    carriage return and a line feed; rows are read back the same way.
  example: the fields a, 'b,c' and 'say "hi"' are written a,"b,c","say ""hi""".
  shows-as: [{call: csv.writer}, {call: csv.reader}, {call: csv.DictWriter}, {call: csv.DictReader}]
  usual: kept
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/csv.html#csv.writer, digest: 963321eeff5f}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/csv.html#csv.Dialect.lineterminator, digest: 963321eeff5f}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Files and paths
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- id: home-and-tilde
  on: platform
  of: runtime
  name: the home folder and ~
  gives: The home folder is the HOME variable when it is set, else the account's entry in the system's password
    database. A path beginning with ~ has it replaced by the home folder (a trailing slash of the home dropped), and
    ~user by that account's home; a user the system does not know is refused with an error saying the home folder
    could not be determined.
  example: with HOME=/home/ann, '~/x' is '/home/ann/x'; '~nobody-here/x' is refused.
  shows-as: [{method: expanduser}, {call: os.path.expanduser}, {call: pathlib.Path.home}, {call: os.path.expandvars}]
  usual: kept
  from: [paper, TS 3.21, SW 3.23, TS 1.28, TS 1.30, SW 1.25, SW 1.26, SW 2.12]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/pathlib.html#pathlib.Path.home, digest: ea319d48765a}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/pathlib.html#pathlib.Path.expanduser, digest: ea319d48765a}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/os.path.html#os.path.expanduser, digest: 2e5a306b1a37}

- id: path-written
  on: platform
  of: runtime
  name: paths written the POSIX way
  gives: A path is written as text with its parts joined by '/', '.' parts and repeated slashes dropped, no trailing
    slash, a leading '//' kept, '..' left as it is, and '.' for an empty path; a relative path stays relative.
  example: "'./a//b/' is written 'a/b'; '' is written '.'."
  shows-as: [{call: pathlib.Path}, {call: pathlib.PurePath}, {call: pathlib.PurePosixPath}, {annotation: Path}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R01, TS 3.22, SW 3.23, TS 1.29, SW 1.27, SW 2.28]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/pathlib.html#pathlib.PurePath, digest: ea319d48765a}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/pathlib.html#general-properties, digest: ea319d48765a}

- id: path-separator
  on: platform
  of: runtime
  name: the path separator of the platform
  gives: Paths joined by the platform's rules use '/' on macOS and Linux and '\' on Windows; joining an absolute path
    drops everything before it.
  example: joining 'a' and '/b' gives '/b'.
  shows-as: [{call: os.path.join}, {attr: os.sep}]
  usual: alike
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/os.path.html#os.path.join, digest: 2e5a306b1a37}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/os.html#os.sep, digest: 7df584c5ef74}

- id: path-resolved
  on: platform
  of: runtime
  name: paths made absolute
  gives: Making a path absolute joins it to the working folder and, when resolved, follows every link and removes '..'
    parts, so the result names the real file.
  example: with alias.py linking to real/m.py, the resolved alias.py is the absolute path of real/m.py.
  shows-as: [{method: resolve}, {method: absolute}, {call: os.path.abspath}, {call: os.path.realpath}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R15]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/pathlib.html#pathlib.Path.resolve, digest: ea319d48765a}

- id: path-relative
  on: platform
  of: runtime
  name: paths shown relative to the working folder
  gives: A path below the working folder can be shown relative to it, by comparing the texts of the two paths part by
    part; a path not below it cannot, and is refused with a value error.
  example: in /home/ann, /home/ann/src/m.py is shown as src/m.py; /tmp/x is refused.
  shows-as: [{method: relative_to}, {call: os.path.relpath}, {call: pathlib.Path.cwd}, {call: os.getcwd}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R31]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/pathlib.html#pathlib.PurePath.relative_to, digest: ea319d48765a}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/pathlib.html#pathlib.Path.cwd, digest: ea319d48765a}

- id: path-kinds
  on: platform
  of: runtime
  name: what a path names
  gives: A path names a file, a folder, something else, or nothing; a broken link names nothing, so asking whether it
    exists says no; a path under a file names nothing too.
  example: a broken link named on the command line is reported as not found.
  shows-as: [{method: exists}, {method: is_file}, {method: is_dir}, {call: os.path.exists}, {call: os.path.isfile},
             {call: os.path.isdir}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R29, SW 1.23]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/pathlib.html#pathlib.Path.exists, digest: ea319d48765a}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/pathlib.html#pathlib.Path.is_file, digest: ea319d48765a}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/pathlib.html#pathlib.Path.is_dir, digest: ea319d48765a}

- id: folder-walk
  on: platform
  of: runtime
  name: walking a folder
  gives: A folder is walked at any depth in the order the file system gives, hidden folders included and linked folders
    not entered; each file keeps the path it was found under.
  example: walking '.', a file reached through a link is named by the link.
  names: ['\brglob\b']
  shows-as: [{method: rglob}, {method: glob}, {method: walk}, {call: os.walk}, {call: glob.glob}, {call: os.scandir},
             {call: os.listdir}, {method: iterdir}]
  usual: alike
  from: [V-R15]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/pathlib.html#pathlib.Path.rglob, digest: ea319d48765a}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/pathlib.html#pathlib.Path.glob, digest: ea319d48765a}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/os.html#os.walk, digest: 7df584c5ef74}
    - nowhere: the order of the walk, which is the file system's.

- id: make-folders
  on: platform
  of: runtime
  name: folders made as needed
  gives: Making a folder with its parents makes every missing folder on the way; asked not to mind one that exists, it
    says nothing when the folder is there, but is still refused when a file stands where a folder should.
  example: a ledger at ~/a/b/x.jsonl makes ~/a and ~/a/b first; a file named ~/a makes it fail.
  shows-as: [{method: mkdir}, {call: os.makedirs}, {call: os.mkdir}]
  usual: kept
  from: [TS 3.27, SW 3.24, TS 1.42, SW 1.28, SW 2.22]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/pathlib.html#pathlib.Path.mkdir, digest: ea319d48765a}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/os.html#os.makedirs, digest: 7df584c5ef74}

- id: file-created-mode
  on: platform
  of: runtime
  name: the permissions of a new file
  gives: A file the program creates gets read and write permission for everyone (0666) less the user's mask, and a new
    folder 0777 less the mask.
  example: with a mask of 022, a new ledger is 0644 and a new folder 0755.
  shows-as: [{call: open, mode: [w, a, x]}, {method: open, mode: [w, a, x]}, {method: write_text}, {method: mkdir}]
  usual: free
  from: [TS 1.42, SW 1.28, SW 3.24]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/os.html#os.mkdir, digest: 7df584c5ef74}
    - nowhere: that a file opened for writing is created with 0666 less the mask.

- id: newlines-translated
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: line ends read and written as text
  gives: A file read as text turns carriage return and line feed, and a lone carriage return, into a line feed; written
    as text, a line feed is written as it is on macOS and Linux and as carriage return and line feed on Windows.
  example: a file holding 'a\r\nb' is read as 'a\nb'.
  shows-as: [{call: open}, {method: read_text}, {method: write_text}, {method: open, import: pathlib}]
  usual: alike
  from: [TS 3.24, SW 3.21, TS 1.24]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#open, digest: 216dc071d8fc}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/io.html#io.TextIOWrapper, digest: 8261a1a54d06}

- id: text-decoding-strict
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: text decoded strictly
  gives: Text decoded as UTF-8 refuses the first byte sequence that is not valid UTF-8 with a decoding error, a kind of
    value error, naming the position; a byte-order mark at the start is kept as the character U+FEFF, unless the
    encoding asked for is the one that drops it.
  example: a ledger holding the byte 0xE9 alone fails to read; a file beginning with a byte-order mark gives a first line
    that begins with U+FEFF.
  shows-as: [{method: read_text}, {method: decode}, {call: open, kw: [encoding]}, {method: open, kw: [encoding]}]
  usual: kept
  from: [TS 1.26, SW 1.21, SW 1.22, SW 2.23, TS 2.17]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/codecs.html#error-handlers, digest: f3b35fcd2d0f}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/codecs.html#encodings-and-unicode, digest: f3b35fcd2d0f}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#bytes.decode, digest: 736b53f803b0}

- id: bytes-decoded
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: bytes and text are different things
  gives: Bytes and text are different kinds and are never mixed silently. Bytes become text only by decoding them with a
    named encoding, which fails on bytes that encoding cannot read; text read from a byte file is decoded once, and a
    file already read to its end gives nothing more.
  example: '"<r>é</r>" in Latin-1 bytes, decoded as UTF-8, fails at the é.'
  names: ['\bbytes\b', '\bcodecs\b', '\bUnicodeDecodeError\b']
  shows-as: [{method: decode}, {annotation: bytes}, {call: bytes}, {call: codecs.decode}, {call: codecs.getreader}]
  usual: kept
  from: [S-R17, S-R18]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#bytes.decode, digest: 736b53f803b0}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/codecs.html#standard-encodings, digest: f3b35fcd2d0f}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/io.html#io.BufferedIOBase.read, digest: 8261a1a54d06}

- id: source-encodings
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: the encoding of a source file
  gives: A program file's encoding is a UTF-8 byte-order mark, else a coding comment on its first or second line, else
    UTF-8; any encoding the runtime knows may be declared; carriage return and line feed, and a lone carriage return,
    are read as a line feed.
  example: a file declaring latin-1 whose name holds the byte 0xE9 reads it as é.
  names: ['\btokenize\b']
  shows-as: [{call: tokenize.open}, {call: tokenize.detect_encoding}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R24]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/tokenize.html#tokenize.open, digest: 9909f187afc9}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/tokenize.html#tokenize.detect_encoding, digest: 9909f187afc9}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: reference/lexical_analysis.html#encoding-declarations, digest: 26b7f8ab2fcf}

- id: package-data
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: files read from inside the installed program
  gives: A data file shipped inside the installed program is read as bytes by its package and relative name, wherever
    the program is installed.
  example: the bundled list for one module is read from inside the program and scanned under its relative name.
  names: ['\bpkgutil\b']
  shows-as: [{call: pkgutil.get_data}, {call: importlib.resources.files}, {call: importlib.resources.read_text}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R28]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/pkgutil.html#pkgutil.get_data, digest: 86fc20a5e589}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Patterns and globs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- id: glob-patterns
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: glob patterns
  gives: In a glob pattern '*' matches any run of characters, '/' included, '?' one character, '[abc]' one of a set and
    '[!abc]' one not in it; there is no escape character, and the whole text must match.
  example: "'*/other/*' matches 'a/other/y.py'; '[ab]/sub1' must match the whole path it is given."
  names: ['\bfnmatch\w*', '\bglob\b']
  shows-as: [{call: fnmatch.fnmatch}, {call: fnmatch.fnmatchcase}, {call: fnmatch.filter}, {call: fnmatch.translate}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R06, V-R07]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/fnmatch.html#module-fnmatch, digest: 7d13693a9cde}

- id: glob-case-per-platform
  on: platform
  of: runtime
  name: case in glob patterns follows the platform
  gives: Matching a path with a glob follows the platform's rule for case, case-sensitive on macOS and Linux and
    case-insensitive on Windows, whatever the program's own words say.
  example: the pattern 'gen' does not match 'Gen/x.py' on macOS.
  shows-as: [{call: fnmatch.fnmatch}, {call: fnmatch.filter}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R05]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/fnmatch.html#fnmatch.fnmatch, digest: 7d13693a9cde}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/os.path.html#os.path.normcase, digest: 2e5a306b1a37}

- id: glob-case-sensitive
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: glob patterns matched case for case
  gives: Matching names with a glob case for case compares exactly, on every platform.
  example: the decorator pattern '@Route' does not match '@route'.
  shows-as: [{call: fnmatch.fnmatchcase}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R07]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/fnmatch.html#fnmatch.fnmatchcase, digest: 7d13693a9cde}

- id: pattern-syntax
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: text patterns
  gives: Text patterns have repeats that are greedy unless marked lazy, look-ahead and look-behind, groups and
    alternation; a search finds the leftmost match, a match anchors at the start or at a given position, and finding
    all matches goes left to right without overlap.
  example: the lazy pattern for a comment stops at the first '-->', so '<!-- a -- b -->' is one comment.
  names: ['\bre\.\w+', '\bregular expression']
  shows-as: [{call: re.compile}, {call: re.search}, {call: re.match}, {call: re.fullmatch}, {call: re.finditer},
             {call: re.findall}, {call: re.sub}, {call: re.split}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R16, S-R01, S-R06]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/re.html#regular-expression-syntax, digest: 17d3cdde81f1}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/re.html#re.search, digest: 17d3cdde81f1}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/re.html#re.finditer, digest: 17d3cdde81f1}

- id: pattern-unicode-classes
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: word, digit and space in patterns are Unicode's
  gives: In a text pattern, a word character is any Unicode letter, digit or underscore, a digit any Unicode decimal
    digit, and white space the set of the entry on white space, unless the pattern is marked ASCII-only.
  example: a word field named 'naïve' is matched whole; U+3000 is matched as white space.
  shows-as: [{call: re.compile, arg0: '\w'}, {call: re.compile, arg0: '\s'}, {call: re.compile, arg0: '\d'},
             {call: re.search, arg0: '\w'}, {call: re.search, arg0: '\s'}, {call: re.match, arg0: '\w'},
             {call: re.match, arg0: '\s'}, {call: re.sub, arg0: '\s'}, {call: re.findall, arg0: '\w'}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R20, S-R02]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/re.html#regular-expression-syntax, digest: 17d3cdde81f1}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/re.html#re.ASCII, digest: 17d3cdde81f1}

- id: pattern-flags
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: pattern flags
  gives: A pattern may ignore case (by Unicode's simple case folding), let '^' and '$' match at every line, and let '.'
    match a line feed; without them, '.' stops at a line feed and '$' matches at the end or before a final line feed.
  example: ignoring case, '# noqa' matches '# NoQA'.
  shows-as: [{attr: re.IGNORECASE}, {attr: re.I}, {attr: re.MULTILINE}, {attr: re.M}, {attr: re.DOTALL}, {attr: re.S}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R16, S-R01]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/re.html#re.IGNORECASE, digest: 17d3cdde81f1}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/re.html#re.MULTILINE, digest: 17d3cdde81f1}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/re.html#re.DOTALL, digest: 17d3cdde81f1}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Formats the standard library reads
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- id: toml-read
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: the TOML file read
  gives: A configuration file is read as TOML 1.0, tables, arrays, true and false, underscores in numbers and escapes
    in strings, into maps, lists, whole numbers, fractional numbers, text, yes-or-no and dates; a file that is not
    valid TOML is refused with a decoding error, a kind of value error.
  example: min_confidence = 1_0 reads as 10; a file holding '[[' is refused.
  names: ['\btomllib\b', '\btomli\b']
  shows-as: [{call: tomllib.load}, {call: tomllib.loads}, {import: tomli}, {import: toml}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R08]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/tomllib.html#module-tomllib, digest: 7e6dc3e667df}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/tomllib.html#conversion-table, digest: 7e6dc3e667df}

- id: html5-entities
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: the HTML5 named references
  gives: The named character references of HTML5 are a fixed table of 2,231 names, legacy ones such as amp, lt and copy
    also without their semicolon, each mapped to one or two characters.
  example: "'&copy' reads as ©; '&notit;' is no name of the table."
  names: ['\bhtml\.entities\b', '\bhtml5\b']
  shows-as: [{attr: html.entities.html5}, {import: html.entities}]
  usual: kept
  from: [S-R07]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/html.entities.html#html.entities.html5, digest: e83ead67b495}

- id: tree-text-and-tail
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: an element tree's text and tail
  gives: In an element tree an element's text is what comes before its first child and each child's tail is what
    follows it up to the next sibling; attributes keep their order.
  example: in '<r>a<b/>c</r>', r's text is 'a' and b's tail is 'c'.
  names: ['\bxml\.etree\b', '\bElementTree\b', '\betree\b']
  shows-as: [{import: xml.etree.ElementTree}, {import: xml.etree}]
  usual: kept
  from: [S-R15]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/xml.etree.elementtree.html#xml.etree.ElementTree.Element,
       digest: 5e516318a17d}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A program that reads programs (the language's own parser, as a library)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- id: source-parsed
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: the input language and its lines
  gives: Program text is read by the grammar of the runtime's own release, no other; every statement and expression
    carries its first and last line and column; a decorated definition carries the line of its def or class, not of
    its first decorator.
  example: the forms of a later release are syntax errors; a function decorated on line 3 and defined on line 4 is on
    line 4.
  shows-as: [{call: ast.parse}, {call: compile, kw: [flags]}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R02, V-R12]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: reference/grammar.html#full-grammar-specification, digest: fe32d00f620e}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/ast.html#ast.parse, digest: c8e0214fb37f}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/ast.html#ast.AST, digest: c8e0214fb37f}
    - nowhere: that a decorated definition's line is its def or class line.

- id: identifiers-normalised
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: names normalised
  gives: Names in program text are normalised to Unicode's NFKC form while it is read, so two names that look different
    may be one, and the name reported is the normalised one.
  example: a function named with a black-letter H is reported as H; 'ﬁle' is the same name as 'file'.
  shows-as: [{call: ast.parse}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R19]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: reference/lexical_analysis.html#2-3-identifiers-and-keywords, digest: 26b7f8ab2fcf}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/unicodedata.html#unicodedata.normalize, digest: 607cc720d0f3}

- id: parser-messages
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: the parser's messages
  gives: A syntax error carries a message, a line and the line's text, all of the runtime's own release; a null byte in
    the input is a syntax error with no line.
  example: a file holding a null byte is reported with no line number.
  shows-as: [{call: ast.parse, handler: SyntaxError}, {handler: SyntaxError}]
  usual: alike
  from: [V-R26]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/exceptions.html#SyntaxError, digest: fd1dcc489d5c}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: whatsnew/3.12.html#other-language-changes, digest: e490fac5414f}
    - nowhere: the parser's messages, and that a null-byte syntax error carries no line.

- id: type-comments
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: type comments read as code
  gives: Asked to, the parser reads type comments as expressions; one that does not parse is an error at the node that
    carries it.
  example: a bad type comment on one function stops the reading at that function.
  shows-as: [{call: ast.parse, kw: [type_comments]}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R27]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/ast.html#ast.parse, digest: c8e0214fb37f}

- id: tree-dump
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: the parser's tree written out
  gives: A tree from the parser can be written out as text, each node by its kind and fields.
  example: a module with one statement is written 'Module(body=[...])'.
  shows-as: [{call: ast.dump}]
  usual: free
  from: [V-R30]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/ast.html#ast.dump, digest: c8e0214fb37f}
    - nowhere: the exact form of the written tree beyond a formatted dump.

- id: literal-truth
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: a literal's value and truth
  gives: A literal (numbers, text, bytes, nothing, the ellipsis, tuples, lists, maps, sets) can be evaluated without
    running anything else, a sign on a number included; its truth follows the entry on which values count as false.
  example: "'-0' evaluates to 0, which is false; 1e400 evaluates to infinity, which is true."
  names: ['\bliteral_eval\b']
  shows-as: [{call: ast.literal_eval}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R10]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/ast.html#ast.literal_eval, digest: c8e0214fb37f}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/stdtypes.html#truth-value-testing, digest: 736b53f803b0}
    - nowhere: that a sign on a number is accepted, and that 1e400 reads as infinity.

- id: program-tree-nodes
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: the kinds of a program's tree
  gives: The parser's tree has one kind of node per construct of the grammar, visited in the order of their fields;
    return, raise, break and continue end the flow of their block.
  example: code after a return in the same block is never reached.
  shows-as: [{base: ast.NodeVisitor}, {base: NodeVisitor}, {call: ast.walk}, {call: ast.iter_child_nodes}]
  usual: kept
  from: [V-R12]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/ast.html#ast.NodeVisitor, digest: c8e0214fb37f}
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: reference/simple_stmts.html#the-return-statement, digest: cf5e83ddb366}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The runtime itself
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- id: runtime-version
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: the runtime's release
  gives: The program runs on the release of its runtime it names and on later releases; what it takes from the runtime
    is that release's behaviour, as this catalogue's citations state it.
  example: a program declaring 3.10 or later runs on 3.13.
  names: ['\bPython (?:\d+(?:\.\d+)*|it needs|release|version)']
  shows-as: [{declared: runtime-version}]
  usual: kept
  from: [TS 2.19, SW 2.1]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/sys.html#sys.version_info, digest: 1fc32ff781c0}

- id: run-as-module
  on: runtime
  of: runtime
  name: a package run by its name
  gives: A package with a main file can be run by its name; the main file runs with the package's other modules
    importable, and its arguments are the words after the package's name.
  example: running the package tally runs tally's main file with the words after 'tally'.
  names: ['\bpython3? -m\b']
  shows-as: [{form: package-main}]
  usual: free
  from: [TS 2.2, SW 2.2, SW 3.26]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: library/__main__.html#main-py-in-python-packages, digest: e24b0d60cf0e}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Assumptions (P:assumption): what the program takes to be true of its surroundings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- id: append-is-whole
  kind: assumption
  on: platform
  of: runtime
  name: one short append lands whole
  about: that a short line appended to a file in one write lands whole, never interleaved with another writer's line
  gives: A short line appended to a file opened for appending is written in one write and lands whole at the end of the
    file, even when another process appends at the same time.
  example: two counts added at the same moment give two whole lines, never one broken line.
  if-false: two writers at once could leave a broken line, which the reader then refuses as not an entry.
  carries: must
  shows-as: [{call: open, mode: [a]}, {method: open, mode: [a]}]
  usual: kept
  from: [paper, TS 1.44, SW 1.51, SW 3.29]
  stated:
    - {in: python, version: 3.13.18, at: builtins/functions.html#open, digest: 216dc071d8fc}
    - nowhere: that a short appended line is issued as one write and lands whole.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dependencies commonly leaned on (on: dependency; their own documents are not in the library)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- id: pytest-conventions
  on: dependency
  of: pytest
  name: the test runner's own names
  gives: The common test runner calls, without being asked, functions named setup_module, teardown_module,
    setup_function and teardown_function, methods named setup_class, teardown_class, setup_method and teardown_method,
    functions named test_ and classes named Test in files whose names mark them as tests; a program that reports unused
    code must not report them.
  example: a function named setup_module in a file named test_x.py is used, though nothing in the file calls it.
  names: ['\bpytest\b']
  shows-as: [{string: [setup_module, teardown_module, setup_function, teardown_function, setup_class, teardown_class,
                       setup_method, teardown_method]}, {import: pytest}]
  usual: kept
  stated:
    - nowhere: the test runner's own documentation is not kept in the language library.
